<header><p class="st-kicker">margraviate · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Northern March</h1><p class="st-meta">965 CE – 1157 CE</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Saxon frontier march east of the Elbe, lost to the Slavs in 983 and revived as the territory that became the March of Brandenburg.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gero-the-great">Margrave Gero</a> died in 965 his huge march was split, and the northern part, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oder-river">Oder</a> north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havel">Havel</a>, became the Northern March. Its first margrave was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dietrich-of-haldensleben">Dietrich of Haldensleben</a>, who ruled over the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-the-hevelli">Hevelli</a> and Lutician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Northern March was a military frontier, defended by castles and supported by the bishoprics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havelberg">Havelberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dietrich-of-haldensleben">Dietrich</a>'s harsh rule helped provoke the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-slav-rising">Great Slav Rising</a> of 983, after which German power collapsed east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> and the margraves held only the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> on the west bank, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salzwedel">Salzwedel</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stendal">Stendal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1134 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothair-i">Lothair</a> III granted the Northern March to the Ascanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albert-the-bear">Albert the Bear</a>. After he secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a> in 1157, he took the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/margrave-of-brandenburg">Margrave of Brandenburg</a>. The old march, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/altmark">Altmark</a> as its western core, became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/margraviate-of-brandenburg">Margraviate of Brandenburg</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/northern-march.jpg" alt="Northern March" width="320" height="276"><figcaption>Image: Skäpperöd · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lutizenbund.PNG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>965 CE – 1157 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>margraviate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Dietrich of Haldensleben, Albert the Bear</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old Saxon, Polabian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Nordmark, Marca Septentrionalis</dd></dl></aside></div>
